{
  "passage": "Every day, every household throws away some waste. Vegetable peels, leftover food, paper, plastic bags, bottles and broken toys all end up in the dustbin. In many towns, the waste is collected by municipal workers and taken to low-lying open areas called landfills. As landfills fill up, they become huge, smelly mountains of garbage that attract flies and rats. Waste can be handled much better if it is separated at home. Kitchen waste such as peels and leftover food is called biodegradable waste, because tiny organisms in the soil can break it down. This waste can be turned into compost, a dark, rich manure, by burying it in a pit for a few weeks. Using earthworms makes the process faster, and this method is called vermicomposting. Plastic, glass and metal are non-biodegradable. They are not broken down by organisms and can remain in the soil for hundreds of years. Many of these materials can be recycled. Paper can be made into new paper, and glass and metal can be melted and reused. Plastic bags are a special problem. When they are thrown away, they block drains, and animals such as cows sometimes eat them and fall ill. Burning plastic releases harmful gases. The best way to deal with plastic is to reduce its use: carry a cloth bag to the market and refuse what you do not need.",
